1
00:00:00,860 --> 00:00:06,230
A canon outgrows any one book. Tolkien's
runs from the Years of the Trees to the

2
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:11,810
Fourth Age, across The Silmarillion,
The Hobbit and The Lord of the Rings.

3
00:00:11,860 --> 00:00:16,800
Here it is on one timeline: each Age a
band, each event tagged with the book

4
00:00:16,850 --> 00:00:18,258
that tells it.

5
00:00:19,748 --> 00:00:23,608
Its years aren't ours, so
the timeline counts its own.

6
00:00:23,658 --> 00:00:27,728
A few lines of display code
print each Age's reckoning,

7
00:00:27,778 --> 00:00:32,708
the Second Age, then the Third, and
read them back when you type a date.

8
00:00:32,758 --> 00:00:36,199
Your world's calendar can
be written the same way.

9
00:00:37,669 --> 00:00:41,559
Follow one thread through
all of it. Separate by tags,

10
00:00:41,609 --> 00:00:46,099
choose the One Ring, and its whole
history rises into a track of its own:

11
00:00:46,149 --> 00:00:50,499
forged in the Second Age, lost for
two and a half thousand years,

12
00:00:50,549 --> 00:00:55,371
found, and carried to its end.
Each step is linked to the next.

13
00:00:56,831 --> 00:01:02,471
A canon is only as good as its sources.
Each event here records which book tells

14
00:01:02,521 --> 00:01:08,371
it and how sure its date is. The fall
of Gondolin's year comes from later

15
00:01:08,421 --> 00:01:13,371
annals, not The Silmarillion itself.
The One Ring's forging is about sixteen

16
00:01:13,421 --> 00:01:18,645
hundred. Disagreements stay visible
instead of being smoothed over.

17
00:01:20,115 --> 00:01:25,635
Zoom from millennia down to single days.
The war that ends the Third Age fills

18
00:01:25,685 --> 00:01:29,455
just over a year, and each day keeps
the calendar the hobbits used,

19
00:01:29,505 --> 00:01:33,241
the Shire Reckoning, beside the Age's own.

20
00:01:34,761 --> 00:01:38,771
Whatever world you're keeping,
keep its canon in one place,

21
00:01:38,821 --> 00:01:41,136
with every date's source beside it.
